 \section{Introduction}
 
-Analog circuit schematics encode functional information (component types, values, and topological connections) in a visual format designed for human interpretation. Converting scanned schematics into machine-readable SPICE (Simulation Program with Integrated Circuit Emphasis) netlists enables automated simulation, design verification, and large-scale circuit analysis. While recent advances in object detection have made component recognition increasingly reliable, the \emph{joining} problem, determining which wire segments connect to which component pins and to each other, remains the primary failure mode in end-to-end digitization pipelines.
+Analog circuit schematics encode component types, values, and topological connections in a visual format designed for human interpretation. Recovering a structural netlist from a scan is a prerequisite for simulation, design verification, and large-scale circuit analysis, but a complete simulatable SPICE deck also requires component values and device models. This work addresses the structural connectivity stage only. While recent advances in object detection have made component recognition increasingly reliable, the \emph{joining} problem, determining which wire segments connect to which component pins and to each other, remains a primary failure mode in digitization pipelines.
 
 The challenge is threefold. First, wire endpoints extracted from binarized images are inherently noisy: the same circuit drawn by different drafters produces endpoint positions that vary by 10--80 pixels. Second, components occupy bounding boxes that overlap with wire endpoints, creating ambiguity about whether an endpoint connects to a component's pin or merely passes nearby. Third, T-junctions, collinear fragments, and crossing wires create complex connectivity patterns that simple radius-based approaches cannot resolve without over-merging.
 
