 We address these challenges with a complete deterministic pipeline whose novel elements are: (1)~an \emph{occlusion-first wire extractor} that masks detected components before binarization and estimates per-segment endpoints, producing a line-segment-with-endpoints representation (rather than tracing nets directly into connected components, as prior systems do) that is what makes graph-based joining possible; (2)~an \emph{endpoint-graph join} model that constructs a graph over both wire endpoints and component pins, with five edge types capturing distinct connectivity patterns; and (3)~a \emph{degree-budget completion} algorithm that identifies and reconnects dropped connections using constrained optimization. We evaluate on the CGHD-1152 dataset~\cite{bayer2021cghd} with both a synthetic error injection framework that enables systematic robustness analysis and a human-verified net-level benchmark.
 
