 \textbf{Edge type 5 (pin--wire-body):} For each component pin $p$ and wire segment $w_j$, if the point-to-segment distance $\text{dist}(p, w_j) \leq \tau_t$ and the projection lands mid-span (not at $w_j$'s endpoints, which are handled by edge type 3), add edge with weight $\text{dist}(p, w_j)$. This binds components that tap onto a passing rail along the wire's body rather than at its end. It recovers connectivity that endpoint-only binding misses, without the all-to-all over-merge of radius grabbing.
 
-The tolerances $\tau_{\text{join}}$, $\tau_{\text{pin}}$, and $\tau_t$ are \emph{scale-relative}: $\tau = k \cdot s$ where $s$ is the characteristic scale (median component diagonal), so a single parameter setting works across circuits of varying size.
+The tolerances $\tau_{\text{join}}$, $\tau_{\text{pin}}$, and $\tau_t$ are \emph{scale-relative}: $\tau = k \cdot s$ where $s$ is the characteristic scale (median component diagonal). The deployed multipliers are fixed before evaluation ($k_{\text{pin}}=0.62$, $k_{\text{join}}=0.30$, and $k_t=0.20$), with pixel clamps of 24--60, 11--28, and 8--20, respectively. This reduces cross-image retuning but is not learned or locally adaptive: a schematic that mixes very large and very small components still shares one $s$. We therefore report a fixed-pixel ablation and treat heterogeneous component scales as a limitation rather than claiming scale invariance (Section~\ref{sec:real_eval}).
 
 \textbf{Component-first pin assignment:} For edge type 3, we use a two-step assignment. First, assign each endpoint to its nearest component by bounding-box distance (0 if inside the bbox, using a generous radius of $\max(\tau_{\text{pin}}, 0.5 \cdot \text{diagonal})$). Second, within the assigned component, select the pin by geometric position: for horizontal components ($w > h$), left pin if endpoint $x <$ center $x$, else right pin; for vertical components ($h > w$), top pin if endpoint $y <$ center $y$, else bottom pin. This handles the common case where endpoints land inside a component's bounding box but far from its pin. Figure~\ref{fig:endpoint_graph} contrasts the legacy radius join with our endpoint-graph model.
 
