-To quantify the separation argued in Section~\ref{sec:vlm_connectivity}, that connectivity is better solved by an explicit geometric model than left to a VLM, we ran a first-party experiment with a strong general VLM (Claude Opus~4.8). To make the comparison fair, the VLM is given exactly what the join receives: the scanned image with the same ground-truth component boxes labelled by index, and is asked to return, as JSON, the sets of component terminals that share an electrical node; we score the resulting component-pairs with the same metric. Each image is read by an independent, context-free model call (one per image), so no answer leaks between circuits, and the human, not the model, remains the verifier of record. On a clean synthetic control (15 authored circuits) the VLM scores F1~=~0.99, confirming it understands the task and symbol set. On all 31 real scans, evaluated against the human-verified net-level ground truth, it reaches micro-F1~=~0.923 (P~=~0.97, R~=~0.88; macro-F1~=~0.949).
+We ran an auxiliary first-party diagnostic with a general VLM (Claude Opus~4.8). The VLM receives the scanned image and \emph{oracle} ground-truth component boxes labelled by index, and returns JSON sets of component terminals that share an electrical node. Each image is read by an independent, context-free model call, and the human remains the verifier of record. On a clean synthetic control the VLM scores F1~=~0.99; on the 31 real scans it scores micro-F1~=~0.923 (P~=~0.97, R~=~0.88; macro-F1~=~0.949).
 
-The VLM thus edges our deterministic join on aggregate (0.923 vs 0.890 micro-F1), but the gap is not statistically significant: a paired bootstrap over the 31 images puts the difference at $+0.033$ with a 95\% CI of $[-0.009, +0.078]$, which includes zero. Two further findings qualify the comparison. First, the VLM's accuracy degrades with circuit complexity: it is exact (F1~=~1.00) on 21 of the 31 circuits, including all the small 3--5-component cases, but on the largest boards it systematically \emph{omits} connections, with recall falling to 0.63--0.68 on the three densest boards, exactly where a netlist error is most costly; its precision otherwise stays high (0.97 micro), so its failure mode is dropped nets rather than shorts. The macro-F1 (0.949) is correspondingly inflated by the many easy small circuits, which is why we report the pair-weighted micro-F1 as primary. Second, the cost and form of the output differ from the geometric pipeline: each image consumes on the order of $10^5$ tokens, the result is free-form text rather than a structured netlist, and nothing in the model guarantees electrical validity (e.g.\ that a two-terminal component is not shorted). Our geometric join is statistically indistinguishable from the VLM on these circuits while being deterministic, two to three orders of magnitude cheaper, and structurally valid by construction: the self-loop guard guarantees that no two-terminal component is emitted shorted. We therefore treat the VLM as an upper reference for what a frontier model can do here, not as a connectivity module to ship: an explicit model reaches the same accuracy and also gives the determinism, low cost, and structural validity a deployed digitizer needs.
+This diagnostic is not a fair end-to-end comparison with the geometric pipeline. Oracle component boxes remove a major source of uncertainty for the VLM, while the reported geometric result uses detected wires and still relies on annotated component boxes to isolate joining. We therefore do not use the difference between 0.923 and 0.890, or its bootstrap interval, to claim equivalence or superiority. The diagnostic only shows that the VLM can produce connectivity under privileged component priors, with free-form output and substantial token cost. As an additional but preliminary check, we ran the geometric pipeline with predicted component boxes at its default detector threshold. On this subset the active-component detector reached F1~=~0.632 and the end-to-end join reached micro-F1~=~0.247. This is a single run without bootstrap intervals, and the detector's held-out CGHD validation score does not transfer directly to these scans; it is reported only to expose the current end-to-end gap, not as a comparison to the VLM. A proper comparison requires matched component inputs and repeated end-to-end evaluation across independent corpora.
 
 \section{Discussion}
+\label{sec:limitations}
 
 The endpoint-graph model resolves ambiguity that radius-based methods cannot: by representing both wire endpoints and component pins as nodes with typed edges, it distinguishes between a wire that \emph{passes near} a component (endpoint--wire-body edge) and one that \emph{connects to} it (endpoint--pin edge with directional preference). The component-first assignment further disambiguates by using the geometric constraint that wires must reach a component's pin, not just its bounding box.
 
-Degree-budget completion addresses the fundamental limitation of detector noise: even a 97.6\% accurate wire detector drops $\sim$2\% of wire endpoints, and those dropped endpoints are precisely the connections that matter most for simulation accuracy. The $b$-matching formulation bounds the damage from mis-detection (each floating pin gets at most one recovery edge), while the self-loop guard prevents the catastrophic short-circuit failures that plague unconstrained approaches.
+Degree-budget completion addresses one consequence of wire-extraction error: dropped endpoints can disconnect otherwise valid topology. The $b$-matching formulation bounds recovery (each floating pin gets at most one recovery edge), while the self-loop guard rejects a class of short-circuit failures. These are structural safeguards, not a guarantee of a correct end-to-end circuit or simulation result.
+
+The human-verified evaluation covers only 31 images. Its per-strategy bootstrap intervals are wide (approximately $\pm$0.03--0.05) and overlap for closely ranked methods, so the results support a controlled ranking in this set, not a universal performance claim. The labels were bootstrapped from a conservative graph join and corrected by one human annotator; independently authored synthetic ground truth reduces, but cannot eliminate, concern about benchmark-construction bias. No per-image annotation timing log was retained. Future expansion should use independently annotated and adjudicated samples, record annotation effort, and deliberately sample drawing styles, capture conditions, component-size mixtures, dense buses, and multi-layer crossings outside CGHD-1152.
+
+The supported output is also narrow. The detector localizes but does not assign pin topology to the 30 rare classes folded into \texttt{other}, including transformers, thyristors, triacs, relays, optocouplers, and similar devices; these are excluded from netlist emission and evaluation. The current pipeline recovers topology only. It neither reads component values nor validates a netlist derived from a real scan in a circuit simulator. A value-recognition stage and an end-to-end simulation study against a reference circuit are required before claiming complete SPICE-netlist generation.
+
+Crossover detection is a separate end-to-end risk. Its validation recall is 70.7\%, and the join itself is class-agnostic: if crossing-wire fragments are geometrically bridged, they can be merged incorrectly. We audited the verified benchmark by suppressing geometric edges formed near annotated crossovers. Eight of the 31 images contain crossovers (13 instances); one image contains a confirmed false merge caused by three stub fragments landing within the join tolerance. This audit documents a real failure mode, but it is too small to estimate its prevalence. Excluding stubs inside a detected crossover box from geometric bridge edges is a candidate mitigation, not an implemented or benchmarked improvement.
 
-The human-verified net-level evaluation, while novel for hand-drawn circuits, currently covers 31 images; the 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals on the per-strategy F1 are correspondingly wide ($\pm$0.03--0.05) and overlap between adjacent strategies, so while the top ranking is consistent and corroborated by the independently authored synthetic suite, broader annotation is needed to separate the closely ranked configurations. The net-level labels were bootstrapped by a conservative endpoint-graph join over perfect wires and then human-corrected; we mitigate the residual concern that this favors graph-based methods by validating on synthetic ground truth that is authored from scratch, where the same ranking holds. The evaluation draws from a single hand-drawn corpus (CGHD-1152); generalization across other drawing styles, capture conditions, and printed schematics remains to be validated, as does extension to circuits containing devices outside the simulatable set (switches, transformers, etc.), which we exclude from the net-level benchmark. The VLM connectivity comparison uses a single frontier model (Claude Opus~4.8) on the same 31 images; evaluating additional models and prompting strategies is future work. Component detection, at mAP@0.5~=~89.0\% and 70.7\% recall on crossovers (Section~\ref{sec:component_detection}), also leaves room for improvement; moreover the \texttt{other} class folds 30 rare device types together, so the detector localizes those devices but cannot name them, and a netlist containing one is incomplete. Excluding drafter\_0 from training avoids domain-shift artifacts from an outlier drawing style, but leaves that style unmodelled. The pipeline recovers circuit \emph{topology}, not component values: simulation accuracy is reported only on the synthetic suite, where values are known by construction, and no netlist derived from a real scan is simulated here. Producing a numerically simulatable netlist from a scan additionally requires reading component values off the image, which we do not attempt; coupling the join to a value-recognition (OCR) stage, as in~\cite{kelly2024digitizing,sina2026}, and validating end-to-end against a reference simulation, as in~\cite{peker2026}, is the natural completion of the pipeline. Finally, the synthetic error model is a first-pass approximation of detector noise rather than a calibrated fit; with the new real-image net-level metric available, calibrating it to the measured detector failure distribution is a natural next step.
+The scale-relative rule is likewise not adaptive parameter learning. Fixed multipliers and pixel clamps reduce manual retuning across images, but a single median scale cannot represent a schematic containing both very large and very small devices. The fixed-pixel ablation in Table~\ref{tab:edge_ablation} is limited to this benchmark; a dedicated mixed-scale and dense-bus study is needed. Finally, the VLM diagnostic uses one model and oracle component annotations, so additional models, matched inputs, and repeated fully autonomous runs are needed before any comparative conclusion can be drawn.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-We present a complete deterministic pipeline for hand-drawn circuit digitization, built on an occlusion-first wire extractor that yields an endpoint representation and a two-stage endpoint-graph join, together with, to our knowledge, the first human-verified net-level connectivity benchmark for hand-drawn circuits. The endpoint-graph join with degree-budget completion is most robust under synthetic error injection (F1~=~0.95 at the highest severity vs.\ 0.36 for the radius baseline), and, paired with a high-precision scale-relative base, attains connectivity micro-F1~=~0.890 on real images against human-verified ground truth, beating the prior completion default (0.83) and every deterministic baseline, including the connected-component net-tracing recipe used by recent systems (0.62) and a Hough-plus-proximity line tracer (0.81), all on identical detected wires. Running the join on perfect wires does not raise micro-F1 at all, showing the join, not wire detection, is the bottleneck. A first-party experiment with a frontier VLM reaches 0.923 on the same images, a difference from our join that is not statistically significant (paired 95\% CI $[-0.009, +0.078]$), but at two to three orders of magnitude higher cost and without structurally valid output, supporting the explicit geometric design. Future work includes scaling the human-verified benchmark, calibrating the synthetic error model to the measured detector distribution, and extending coverage to devices outside the simulatable set.
+We present a deterministic method for recovering structural component connectivity from hand-drawn schematics, built on occlusion-first wire extraction and an endpoint-graph join with degree-budget completion. On a 31-image human-verified subset of CGHD-1152, the scale-relative completion join reaches micro-F1~=~0.890 and exceeds the deterministic baselines evaluated on identical detected wires. The synthetic suite supplies complementary controlled robustness evidence, and the added ablations describe which mechanisms are distinguishable on the present benchmark. These results are limited to one corpus and to structural connectivity: they do not demonstrate complete SPICE-netlist generation, broad drawing-style generalization, or reliable operation on dense multi-layer schematics. Future work is to expand and independently annotate the benchmark, measure end-to-end component-detection and crossover effects, learn or localize scale handling, add component-value recognition, and validate complete output through simulation.
 
